import{_ as o,o as t,c as s,a2 as r}from"./chunks/framework.BS0bAV7E.js";const u=JSON.parse('{"title":"FAQ - Open Cube OS","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"root/FAQ.md","filePath":"root/FAQ.md"}'),a={name:"root/FAQ.md"};function n(d,e,i,c,l,h){return t(),s("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[r(`<h1 id="faq-open-cube-os" tabindex="-1">FAQ - Open Cube OS <a class="header-anchor" href="#faq-open-cube-os" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;FAQ - Open Cube OS&quot;">​</a></h1><p>Frequently asked questions, answered from the real code base. Where a limit is quoted, the header that defines it is named.</p><h2 id="project" tabindex="-1">Project <a class="header-anchor" href="#project" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Project&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>What is Open Cube OS?</strong> A from-scratch x86_64 kernel (&quot;L0&quot;) plus a WinRE-style recovery shell (the <code>oc&gt;</code> prompt). It boots from a hybrid BIOS+UEFI ISO, brings up its own memory management, scheduler, VFS, network stack and drivers, and gives you 233 shell commands to inspect and control the machine.</p><p><strong>What is this &quot;L0 / L1&quot; thing?</strong> Two-layer architecture:</p><ul><li><strong>L0</strong> = this repository: the complete kernel plus the recovery shell.</li><li><strong>L1</strong> = upper-layer extensions. L0 exposes 162 numbered extension interfaces (plus a documented set of driver/OTA/watchdog/health headers); L1 modules are <em>not</em> shipped with L0.</li></ul><p>The design principle is &quot;everything is extensible&quot; - the shell, TUI, drivers and even the console font engine are extension surfaces.</p><p><strong>Is it Linux?</strong> No. Not a fork, not a distro. Every line of the kernel (140,672 lines across kernel/boot/drivers/fs/net/shell/l1/tui/chinese/arch/userprogs/libs) is original code under Apache 2.0. It does not run Linux binaries; it runs its own small ELF user programs (see <code>userprogs/</code> and the <code>run</code> command).</p><p><strong>What license?</strong> Apache 2.0 (see <code>LICENSE</code>). Every source file carries an <code>SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0</code> header.</p><h2 id="booting" tabindex="-1">Booting <a class="header-anchor" href="#booting" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Booting&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>How do I boot it?</strong></p><div class="language- vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang"></span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span>qemu-system-x86_64 -m 512 -cdrom opencube-&lt;version&gt;.iso</span></span></code></pre></div><p>or write the ISO to a USB stick and boot real hardware. The ISO is hybrid: it boots through legacy BIOS (El Torito) and UEFI (an EFI boot image) alike - <code>make run-bios</code> / <code>make run-uefi</code> do the QEMU variants.</p><p><strong>Does it need a hard disk?</strong> No. The ISO boots self-contained (the kernel is attached as a multiboot2 module). A disk only matters if you want the A/B update slots, /etc persistence volume, or the <code>install</code> command&#39;s target disk.</p><p><strong>Why does the boot menu show slot A / slot B / slot ISO?</strong> That is the A/B update scheme (<code>kernel/ota/ota_ab.{c,h}</code>). GRUB reads a boot-slot flag and boots the active slot; <code>abcfg</code>/<code>ab_partition_test</code> and the OTA commands control it from inside the system.</p><h2 id="the-shell" tabindex="-1">The shell <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-shell" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The shell&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>How many commands?</strong> 233 (verify live: <code>help -a</code> inside the guest, or <code>make api-docs</code> and read <code>docs/data/commands.json</code>).</p><p><strong>Where is the full command list?</strong><code>help -a</code> (alphabetical), <code>help -w</code> (by work-package), <code>help</code> (short list). The reference documentation under <code>docs/manual/</code> covers every domain.</p><p><strong>Why are all system strings English?</strong> The console and kernel paths are ASCII by design (see <code>CONTRIBUTING.md</code> ground rule 1); a separate Chinese console/TUI layer exists (<code>chinese/</code> tree, <code>chinese</code> command), but the system&#39;s own strings stay ASCII for hardware compatibility.</p><h2 id="limits-and-design-choices" tabindex="-1">Limits and design choices <a class="header-anchor" href="#limits-and-design-choices" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Limits and design choices&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>How much RAM does it need?</strong> It runs in 512 MiB QEMU guests comfortably. The physical memory manager parses the multiboot2 memory map and manages whatever the bootloader reports; there is no fixed ceiling in the PMM.</p><p><strong>Why 100 Hz timer?</strong> The PIT is programmed at 100 Hz (<code>kernel/core/core_timer.h</code>) - 10 ms ticks drive the scheduler&#39;s round-robin slices and the soft-timer wheel. HPET and the TSC are additionally supported as time sources (<code>hpet</code>, <code>pmtimer</code>, <code>tsc</code> commands) but the PIT remains the tick source.</p><p><strong>Can commands be removed?</strong> No - the interface contract says the command surface only grows (<code>SHELL_MAX_COMMANDS</code> is 256 with 233 registered, headroom for 23 more; the limit lives in <code>shell/shell.h</code>).</p><p><strong>Where do my files go?</strong><code>ramfs</code> (in-memory, volatile) by default; FAT32 / exFAT / ext4 volumes you mount or format (<code>mkfs.fat32</code> etc.) are persistent; the 32 MiB <code>/etc</code> volume holds config across reboots (<code>etc.img</code>, <code>opencube.conf</code>).</p><h2 id="networking" tabindex="-1">Networking <a class="header-anchor" href="#networking" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Networking&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>What does the network stack do?</strong> Ethernet + ARP + IPv4 + ICMP + UDP + DNS + TCP (with congestion control), sockets for user programs, an HTTPS client (TLS 1.2/1.3 with RSA/EC certificate verification), an SSH client, and an SSH <em>server</em> (<code>sshd</code> on the guest, keys on the /etc volume). The NIC layer supports e1000/e1000e/igb/ixgbe/rtl8139/rtl8169(rtl8168/8125/810x families) and BCM57xx cards; QEMU&#39;s default <code>-device e1000</code> works out of the box.</p><p><strong>How do I get network in QEMU?</strong></p><div class="language- vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang"></span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span>qemu-system-x86_64 -m 512 -cdrom opencube.iso \\</span></span>
<span class="line"><span>    -netdev user,id=n1 -device e1000,netdev=n1</span></span></code></pre></div><p>then inside the guest: <code>dhcp</code>, <code>ping 10.0.2.2</code>, <code>wget 10.0.2.2</code>, <code>dns example.com</code>.</p><h2 id="debugging" tabindex="-1">Debugging <a class="header-anchor" href="#debugging" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Debugging&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>How do I see the kernel log?</strong><code>dmesg</code> (ring buffer), <code>logcat</code> (live follow), <code>logctl</code> (level control and filters) - the logging subsystem is <code>kernel/lib/lib_klog.{c,h}</code> with rotation (<code>kernel.log</code>, <code>kernel.log.1</code>, ... on the /etc volume).</p><p><strong>It triple-faults / reboots on my machine - what now?</strong> Boot with <code>-serial file:log.txt</code> and read the last self-test line that did NOT print <code>OK</code>; that is the failing subsystem. The crash recovery path (<code>crashtest</code>, <code>dumpinfo</code>, crashlog on the /etc volume) captures panics with register dumps. <code>tools/run_fix4_regression.py</code> shows the expected boot sequence in full.</p><p><strong>How do I build with debug symbols?</strong> The default build already carries <code>-g</code> and links, then strips debug symbols from the final ELF; build artifacts before <code>strip</code> remain in <code>build/</code> during the pipeline (<code>opencube_base.elf</code>).</p><h2 id="build-reproducibility" tabindex="-1">Build &amp; reproducibility <a class="header-anchor" href="#build-reproducibility" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Build &amp; reproducibility&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Are builds reproducible?</strong> Yes - <code>make reproducible-check</code> builds the kernel twice from clean and byte-compares the ELF; it must print <code>PASS</code>. There are no <code>__DATE__</code>/<code>__TIME__</code>/<code>__TIMESTAMP__</code> macros in the tree, link order is fixed, and <code>strip</code> removes path-bearing sections. The source zip is a deterministic <code>git archive</code> (tree order, commit mtimes, index permissions). The OTA package embeds the build day (UTC) - it is reproducible within a day, by design.</p><p><strong>Why does CI matter for a hobby kernel?</strong> The CI gate (<code>.github/workflows/build.yml</code>) is the portable truth: a stricter GCC or a newer binutils on the runner once caught three real portability defects that local toolchains silently accepted. The gate runs the exact targets you run locally.</p><p><strong>Windows?</strong><code>build.ps1</code> drives the same pipeline through WSL2 (recommended) or an MSYS2 toolchain - see <code>BUILDING.md</code>.</p><h2 id="extending" tabindex="-1">Extending <a class="header-anchor" href="#extending" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Extending&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>How do I write an L1 extension?</strong> Include the surface headers (<code>l1/l1_ext.h</code>, <code>l1/l1_wp8a.h</code>, ..., <code>tui/tui_ext.h</code>, the driver/OTA/watchdog/health headers), implement your module, and register it. The 162 numbered interfaces and their groupings are in <code>INTERFACES.md</code>; the extension guide lives under <code>docs/l1/</code>.</p><p><strong>Can I add my own shell command from a user program?</strong> Yes - <code>shell_register_command_ex</code> is exposed through the L1 surface (<code>l1_ext_shell_register_command_ex</code>), and the dynamic loader supports L1 shared objects (<code>.so</code> via <code>libs/ld_so.c</code>, try <code>so_test</code> and <code>dlsym_test</code> in the guest).</p>`,40)])])}const m=o(a,[["render",n]]);export{u as __pageData,m as default};
